{"h":3395,"w":2400,"lines":[{"x":0.79170378181544432,"y":0.10184580342220428,"w":0.079191837310791047,"h":0.016222313520343978,"c":1,"t":"Reading"},{"w":0.76954732259114578,"c":1,"y":0.14389534906769796,"h":0.017470964132967226,"t":"dominant laughs when they were in the 'powerful' role of teasers. Dominant laughter was higher","x":0.088477366070682226},{"x":0.08641974638486398,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"in pitch, louder, and more variable in tone than submissive laughter.","w":0.54320988972981765,"y":0.16279069752753128,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.1946452008205064,"t":"A random group of volunteers then listened to an equal number of dominant and submissive","x":0.088414102198284564,"h":0.017541418648440055,"w":0.73673985799153641},{"t":"laughs from both the high- and low-status individuals, and were asked to estimate the social status","h":0.017472676590558955,"c":1,"y":0.21220930216590672,"x":0.086419760489645989,"w":0.78395060221354174},{"c":1,"w":0.77791254679361987,"x":0.086353234388517996,"h":0.018889650720027906,"y":0.22803624223226859,"t":"of the laugher. In line with predictions, laughers producing dominant laughs were perceived to be"},{"c":1,"t":"significantly higher in status than laughers producing submissive laughsỊẠỊiThis was particularly","x":0.086361095063335072,"y":0.24405398932011002,"w":0.75939081827799471,"h":0.019291633992369217},{"w":0.77380325317382803,"h":0.019266690185690716,"c":1,"x":0.086346790952578092,"t":"true for low-status individuals, who were rated as significantly higher in status when displaying a","y":0.26120805538177094},{"w":0.77160491943359388,"h":0.017455556507806946,"c":1,"t":"dominant versus submissive laugh,' Oveis and colleagues note.iThus, by strategically displaying","x":0.08847737551920877,"y":0.27906976728161981},{"h":0.018374804753998886,"c":1,"x":0.086364061094195768,"y":0.2949222149670272,"t":"more dominant laughter when the context allows, low-status individuals may achieve higher","w":0.73673385620117182},{"w":0.75946589152018229,"t":"status in the eyes of others.? However, high-status individuals were rated as high-status whether","x":0.086311925239163728,"y":0.31079198992138157,"h":0.020462633723785872,"c":1},{"w":0.60303095499674486,"y":0.32819241408848576,"c":1,"t":"they produced their natural dominant laugh or tried to do a submissive one.","x":0.08634531498897062,"h":0.017769511372237146},{"w":0.72853866577148441,"h":0.019061689522858916,"c":1,"y":0.3603281219192449,"t":"Another study, conducted by David Cheng and Lu Wang of Australian National University,","x":0.086351665511793005},{"x":0.086359522391926,"y":0.37769939084809123,"c":1,"w":0.75731826782226563,"t":"was based on the hypothesis that humour might provide a respite from tedious situations in the","h":0.018642007672744487},{"c":1,"h":0.018853734603899719,"y":0.39362315444343099,"t":"workplace. Thisimental break' might facilitate the replenishment of mental resources. To test this","x":0.088421315799617442,"w":0.77995010375976559},{"y":0.41279069752856357,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"theory, the researchers recruited 74 business students, ostensibly for an experiment on perception.","w":0.78189300537109374,"c":1,"x":0.086419752351961088},{"t":"First, the students performed a tedious task in which they had to cross out every instance of the","x":0.08641976422991339,"w":0.75925923665364581,"y":0.42873969376705001,"c":1,"h":0.016027748233718886},{"t":"letter 'e' over two pages of text. The students then were randomly assigned to watch a video","x":0.08840426277246706,"w":0.73058848063151038,"h":0.01679515052319136,"c":1,"y":0.44457290592813381},{"h":0.017602793600309141,"t":"clip eliciting either humour, contentment, or neutral feelings. Some watched a clip of the BBC","w":0.75319315592447911,"c":1,"x":0.086363678317932416,"y":0.46202596824772524},{"x":0.086419740734810374,"h":0.017441860778592999,"t":"comedy Mr: Bean, others a relaxing scene with dolphins swimming in the ocean, and others a","c":1,"w":0.74691360473632806,"y":0.47965116263111862},{"x":0.086255718621669211,"h":0.017763065365093755,"t":"factual video about the management profession.","y":0.49484222007138601,"c":1,"w":0.38715708414713551},{"w":0.73045267740885422,"t":"The students then completed a task requiring persistence in which they were asked to guess","x":0.086419751668423431,"y":0.52906976729461486,"h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1},{"t":"the potential performance of employees based on provided profiles, and were told that making","w":0.75102879842122394,"c":1,"x":0.086419765835684073,"y":0.54505300347228391,"h":0.017446995904785489},{"t":"10 correct assessments in a row would lead to a win. However, the software was programmed","y":0.562462731186544,"c":1,"w":0.74289250691731767,"h":0.016182948899915006,"x":0.090475905932777517},{"t":"such that it was nearly impossible to achieve 10 consecutive correct answers. Participants were","h":0.016799041604265597,"w":0.75525904337565108,"y":0.57793588871292401,"c":1,"x":0.088420701358643702},{"w":0.74279836018880219,"h":0.01598837238037687,"y":0.59593023241185872,"t":"allowed to quit the task at any point. Students who had watched the Mr. Bean video ended up","c":1,"x":0.086419749829795667},{"t":"spending significantly more time working on the task, making twice as many predictions as the","x":0.086419764245540778,"w":0.75925923665364581,"h":0.015991796172268868,"y":0.61336866908431387,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.62878366026454835,"h":0.016693315741872605,"x":0.08798827027859181,"t":"other two groups.","w":0.14503421783447262},{"h":0.017441860778592888,"w":0.69341562906901044,"x":0.086419757379381207,"y":0.66279069751508479,"t":"Cheng and Wang then replicated these results in a second study, during which they had","c":1},{"y":0.67873969353933838,"x":0.086419772941683473,"h":0.017481236070289174,"t":"participants complete long multiplication questions by hand. Again, participants who watched the","w":0.77983535766601564,"c":1},{"t":"humorous video spent significantly more time working on this tedious task and completed more","w":0.76555557250976558,"h":0.018629122398205689,"c":1,"x":0.086349643428877701,"y":0.69474631567315603},{"y":0.71242830966122173,"w":0.55776565551757806,"t":"questions correctly than did the students in either of the other groups.","h":0.017411352186798346,"x":0.086350913490988626,"c":1},{"h":0.019195374667433818,"x":0.086340844383443124,"y":0.74542385815595469,"w":0.74499445597330727,"t":"'Although humour has been found to help relieve stress and facilitate social relationships, the","c":1},{"c":1,"t":"traditional view of task performance implies that individuals should avoid things such as humour","w":0.77366256713867176,"x":0.088477358297902153,"y":0.76295641951060977,"h":0.014723203769140158},{"y":0.77906976769994563,"x":0.086419765669548093,"t":"that may distract them from the accomplishment of task goals,'Cheng and Wang conclude. 'We","w":0.76543207804361979,"h":0.015988371257085632,"c":1},{"y":0.79505300311120586,"t":"suggest that humour is not only enjoyable but more importantly, energising?","h":0.018900484864647127,"c":1,"x":0.086419761498925329,"w":0.61111109415690112},{"c":1,"y":0.91860465144311332,"w":0.030864197413126626,"x":0.84156378605240212,"h":0.017441859655301539,"t":"45"}]}